  "style_block": "STYLE (fixed for every image in this series). Medium: Flat 2D digital illustration in a bold-outline Indian cartoon and graphic-novel look, like a clean still from a modern 2D animated series. Thick, even, pure black (#111111) outlines of constant weight around every character and foreground object, about 6 pixels wide on a 941-pixel-wide image; interior detail lines (folds, facial features, seams) about half that weight; background objects outlined in thinner night-navy (#0F1528) lines so the characters stand out. Edges are clean and vector-like with slightly rounded corners: no sketchy, scratchy, hatched or pencil-textured lines. Cel shading only: every colour area is one flat base tone plus one hard-edged shadow shape, with at most one small hard-edged highlight shape; no gradients, no airbrush, no soft blending on any person or object. Not anime, not chibi, not 3D, not photoreal. DRAWING IDIOM (as important as the wardrobe). The series is drawn like a grounded Indian graphic novel for grown-ups: real people observed from life, then simplified into clean flat shapes with bold outlines. Every person is a believable adult in a restrained, semi-realistic idiom, never a rubbery comedy cartoon: nothing is inflated, squashed or balloon-like. Adults stand about seven heads tall (short or stout people about six and a half); heads, noses, ears and hands are normal size for the body, and hands have five fingers. Eyes are as small as real eyes: a solid dark-brown iris under a short black upper-lid line, one tiny white highlight, and only a little white showing at the sides; never large white disc eyes, never bulging or googly eyes. Character comes from silhouette, posture, eyebrows and mouth, not from exaggerated anatomy. Palette: a limited, dusty small-town palette; stay inside it apart from skin tones, wood and tiny prop details: sandy khaki (#C2A56B, shadow #8F7646) uniforms, dark teal (#1F6F6B, shadow #124846) epaulettes and patches, off-white (#F3EAD7), worn terrazzo and plaster greys and creams, dark wood (#5A3A24), brick red (#B3332B), brass (#C9972E), steel grey (#7C8794, shadow #4A5361), tungsten yellow (#F2B33D) for bulbs, deep blue shadow (#1B2440) and night navy (#0F1528). Reserved colours, each used only on its own thing: cherry red (#D7263D) only on Nandini's helmet; bright orange (#F28C1E) only on Pushpa's wool, her phone cover and the almirah-key loop; plum (#6B3A6E) only on Pushpa's cardigan; marigold (#F4A51C) only on the garland; pink (#E9A3B5) only on the movement slip; spotless white (#FBFAF5) only on Bhaiyaji's kurta and pyjama; mustard (#D9A62E) only on Nandini's kurta in the last image of branch B. The truck cab is a dull ochre (#B5652B), never bright orange. Light: every story frame names its light in a LIGHT line; draw only the light sources it names. Light is drawn as flat shapes: a lit area is one flat lighter tone with a hard edge, and a rim light is one hard-edged flat band of the light's colour, never a gradient glow. Shafts of window light and the cones of bulbs, lamps and headlights are drawn as semi-transparent flat shapes with soft edges; that is part of the look, and it is the only soft-edged element allowed. People, objects and cast shadows keep hard cel edges. By day, shadows are flat dusty violet-grey (#6F6A7E) shapes; at night, flat deep-blue (#1B2440) and night-navy (#0F1528) shapes; never a pure black fill and never grey murk. Faces, hands and the key prop of the shot always stay clearly readable. Camera: eye level or slightly below, natural perspective like a 35 to 50 mm lens (wide shots no wider than about 28 mm); no fisheye, no extreme distortion, no tilted horizon, no depth-of-field blur. Depth comes from overlapping flat layers: a darker, simpler foreground shape, the subject, then a simpler background. Composition: vertical 9:16, full bleed to all four edges, one single image of one moment. Faces and the main subject sit inside the central 75 percent of the frame; nothing important in the bottom 20 percent (subtitles are placed there later) or within 8 percent of any edge (the frame is cropped slightly for camera moves). Finish: clean flat colour, crisp edges, no film grain, no paper texture, no halftone dots, no glow blur, no lens effects.",
  "negative_rules": "STRICT RULES. No text of any kind anywhere in the image: no letters, numbers, words, signs, captions, labels, speech bubbles, subtitles, sound-effect lettering, name tags, nameplates, file labels, calendars, clock numerals or writing on paper, in Devanagari, Latin or any other script; boards, banners, tokens, tags, slips, registers, files, the shield plate and phone screens are blank or carry plain shapes only, and clocks have hands and no numerals. No watermark, no signature, no logo, no brand mark, no user-interface overlay. No real-world police, military or government insignia, crests, badges, stars, flags or emblems of any country; no national flag, no national emblem, no portraits or framed pictures on the walls, no religious symbols or marks, no party colours. The only emblem allowed is the fictional one: a simple white hurricane lantern on a round dark-teal patch. Rank is shown only by plain off-white bars or one broad band on the teal epaulettes. Nobody wears a cap or hat except Nirala, whose khaki peaked cap has no badge. No real or recognisable people. No guns, no real weapons, no blood; the only weapon-shaped object is Nirala's carved wooden revolver, which is holstered, lying on a surface or held in profile, and is never pointed at a face or at the camera. No crowd, no children. No gradients, no photographic texture, no 3D rendering, no lens flare, no motion blur, no film grain, no soft-focus blur. No comic panels, borders, frames, letterbox bars, split screens or speed lines. No extra people, no duplicated characters, no extra fingers or limbs. No change to the locked wardrobe.",
