  "usage_notes": [
    "Required fields as in the proof: style_block, negative_rules, palette, characters, locations, key_props. Extra fields (lens, grade, heights_cm, pairs, sheets, gate lists, asset_order, not_drawn_as_props) are guidance for S4, S5, S7, S8 and the visual checker.",
    "Generation order is asset_order (also refs/prompts/order.txt: id then the ids of its refs). Each asset's refs name only assets earlier in the list. The three worn props (medal, wooden_revolver, godown_keys) come first because the Nirala and Bhaiyaji sheets attach them, as the proof's Bablu sheet attached the torch. Period sheets attach their approved period 1 sheet; pair sheets attach the two approved individual sheets; plates attach the prop sheets of objects that stand in them. Regenerate a dependent asset if the asset it references is regenerated.",
    "Every sheet, plate and prop prompt is complete and self-contained: run the .txt verbatim with the listed refs in the listed order, and do not append style_block or negative_rules. Each prompt still works if run with no refs.",
    "Story frame prompt order (S7): the opening line 'Use your image generation tool to create ONE new vertical 9:16 image. Pass the full description below to the image tool without shortening or paraphrasing it.'; the reference labels in refs order ('Image 1 is the height line-up sheet for Nandini and Nirala...'); the shot description, naming what is visible rather than camera jargon; the STATE block from the continuity file (D-013 rule 3); for every principal visible, character_lock then the wardrobe_locks entry for the period of that beat, verbatim; the pair's two_shot_rule when both are in frame; the grade LIGHT line for the beat; style_block; negative_rules.",
    "References, at most 5 a call (D-013 rule 1): the sheet of each principal in shot for the right period (or the pair sheet, which counts once for two people), the location plate, the prop sheets of props visible in the shot by priority, and the scene anchor. Over 5: drop the plate first when the anchor shows the same setting, then priority 3 and 2 props, then props already drawn on a sheet (medal, holstered revolver, godown keys, peanut cone). No more than two sheet faces in a frame (D-016 direction 14).",
    "Pair sheets: priority 1 pairs are named in the bible text in one image; priority 2 pairs only share beats, so S8 makes them only if the storyboard puts both in one frame. A pair sheet fixes height and bulk; when a beat is in another period (Nirala 1b or 2, Nandini 2) attach the period sheet as well as, or instead of, the pair sheet, and keep the two_shot_rule sentence. Without a pair sheet, the two_shot_rule sentence is still required.",
    "Nirala's periods (S6 decision on D-006 change 20): period 1b gets its own sheet (nirala_p1b) because the period 1 sheet shows the medal, and an attached medal is copied into frames. Period 2 covers all of s2b03, court included: the bible does not say what he wears in the dock, and the cap, medal and revolver are exhibits, so S6 put him in the plain off-white shirt for the whole episode. If S4 or S5 wants him in the uniform without cap in court, prompt from nirala_p1b's uniform with the p2 bare head and log it.",
    "Pushpa's cardigan is drawn sleeveless so that her one-bar epaulettes and lantern patches stay visible over the uniform; the bible does not specify sleeves. Her knitting work is orange wool on two pale bamboo needles (pushpa_knitting); the two steel needles stay crossed in her bun at all times.",
    "Left and right in every lock are the character's own. Write sides in shots in picture terms (D-013 rule 3): front view, Nandini's pouch and Nirala's holster are frame-left of their bodies; Nandini's pen, Nirala's medal, Tripathi's gamchha and cone, Bhaiyaji's watch and the DSP's cane are frame-right; Bhaiyaji's keys are frame-left. s2a02 chain: her LEFT wrist to his RIGHT wrist, so in a front two-shot she stands picture-left and he picture-right with the chain between them (bible production_locks).",
    "Text: all readable text is an assembly overlay (bible overlays). Boards, tokens, envelope tags, the slip (no signature or stamp painted; ov_slip_signature carries them), the register margin, the shield plate, banners and phone screens are painted blank; clocks have hands and no numerals. The talkies clock is on the back wall left of the board (D-016 direction 17); the thana clock is above the front doorway.",
    "Light: use grade.beat_light for each beat; where a beat changes light inside itself the entry says so. A plate drawn in one light state (plate_light_state) is a layout reference for its other states: the shot prompt says 'Image N is the location plate, drawn by day; this shot is at night: keep the layout and change the light as the LIGHT line says'. Soft-edged cones and shafts are accepted; gradient rim light on people is not (D-008).",
    "Reserved colours (palette_names) are reserved so their objects pop and do not leak: cherry red only on the helmet, bright orange only on Pushpa's wool things, pink only on the slip, marigold only on the garland, spotless white only on Bhaiyaji. If a frame shows a reserved colour elsewhere, the visual checker logs it.",
    "Minor faces have no sheets (bible minor_characters): door1_man, door2_woman and door3_man are described in their one shot from the bible's look line and never recur; magistrate, defence lawyer, door4 voice, reporter, tea-stall keeper and the truck driver are hands, backs or voices only. No crowd, no children.",
    "Weapon rule (D-004): the wooden revolver is holstered, lying on a surface in profile or held in profile (butt first in s2b02 b11); never pointed at a face or the camera. If Codex refuses a revolver frame, the frame runner reports it (exit 4) and the storyboard artist restages; do not reword here.",
    "Not produced by S6: the reference images. S8 runs these prompts with production/scripts/codex-frame.sh into content/series2/refs/; the visual checker then checks each gate list. No prompt here has been run, and none of these locks has been checked against an image."
